**Read:** [Functional material response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM), the complete Dielectrics package; [Electrical, Optical, and Magnetic Properties of Materials — MIT 3.23](../sources/Functional-Materials-MIT-3-23.lingua#r_3TPN5CK13R844S7PGFK7C1STVN), dielectric media and optical response. Then follow the shared polarization, response-model and lossy-medium Records.

Electronic displacement, ionic motion, molecular orientation and interfacial charge accumulation operate on different scales. A measured permittivity depends on which mechanisms can respond at the measurement frequency. Local fields, specimen geometry and boundary conditions connect microscopic dipoles with macroscopic polarization.

Define whether ε denotes absolute or relative permittivity and state the harmonic time convention. For exp(−iωt), a passive scalar dielectric has positive imaginary permittivity in the usual local representation. Conductivity can be included as iσ/ω in absolute effective permittivity; adding it again as an independent loss would double count that contribution.

**Work:** Compare a polymer film, a ceramic capacitor and a porous composite at matched temperature, frequency and field amplitude. Explain what a high low-frequency apparent permittivity might reveal about interfaces rather than intrinsic polarization. Derive the measurement model before interpreting its fitted constant.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
